            <ArticleTitle>Chest wall angiolipoma complicating von Recklinghausen disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e73-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.athoracsur.2013.03.107</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0003-4975(13)00906-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present the case of an 18-year-old man with chest wall angiolipoma and a medical history of von Recklinghausen neurofibromatosis. The chest wall tumor was originally detected during an evaluation for chest pain. For diagnostic and therapeutic purposes, video-assisted thoracoscopic resection was performed, and the tumor was histopathologically confirmed to be an angiolipoma. Chest wall angiolipoma is exceptionally rare. Only two cases have been reported in the English literature, with no reports regarding chest wall angiolipoma in a patient with von Recklinghausen disease.</AbstractText>
